message.content:
Here’s the GraphQL query you’d send to the API endpoint (e.g., `https://api.example.com/graphql`).  
Make sure to include your API key in the request header as an authorization bearer token.

```graphql
{
  communityComments(domain: "test4.org", cursor: "cur456") {
    edges {
      node {
        id
        context
      }
    }
    pageInfo {
      hasNextPage
      endCursor
    }
  }
}
```

**Request example (curl)**

```bash
curl -X POST https://api.example.com/graphql \
     -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
     -H "Authorization: Bearer key123" \
     -d '{"query":"{ communityComments(domain: \"test4.org\", cursor: \"cur456\") { edges { node { id context } } pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor } } }"}'
```

This will return only the `id` and `context` fields for each comment, along with pagination info (`hasNextPage`, `endCursor`) so you can continue fetching more pages if needed.

